Semi-realistic 3D medical-illustration still of ONE human Type IIa skeletal muscle fibre (fast oxidative-glycolytic fibre), centered in a square 1:1 1080x1080 frame with generous margin on a clean seamless dark studio background that fills the frame edge to edge. NO border, frame, mat, vignette or letterbox bar, and NOT rendered as a picture lying on a surface. Soft global illumination, gentle rim light, subtle subsurface scattering through the translucent sarcolemma, scientific-animation look.

FRAMING (critical, this failed badly on a sibling render — fix it from the start): the fibre must be drawn SO LARGE AND SO ZOOMED IN that it is physically impossible to fit an end-cap inside the canvas. Do NOT draw a rounded end-cap or closed disc AT ALL, anywhere, under any circumstances — not even partially. The tube's two long straight edges must run from one corner of the canvas to the opposite corner, filling the diagonal, with the tube's own body touching or crossing all four sides of the square canvas near the corners. Picture a fat diagonal band of tube-body filling most of the frame, so wide and so close to the viewer that both of its ends are pushed completely outside the visible picture — you should not be able to see where the tube starts or stops, only its long straight sides and the banded surface between them. If you can see background beyond either tip of the tube, the tube is drawn too small — make it at least 40% larger and re-crop tighter around its middle section. It never forks or tapers into a Y. It reads clearly as a MID-SIZED fibre — narrower than the widest of the three human fibre types, wider than the narrowest — and its overall colour, in NATURAL, believable biological tones, is a warm, translucent PALE ROSE-PINK, visibly pinker/deeper than a pale-cream fibre and clearly lighter than a deep-red fibre; never grey, neon, or colourless.

BAND DENSITY: draw at least TWENTY narrow cross-bands along the visible length, closely spaced like fine engraving lines or ridges — not four or five wide stripes. The bands should look numerous and fine, repeating rapidly along the tube, the way real sarcomere striation looks under magnification: a dense fine ladder/comb pattern, not a few thick painted rings.

CROSS-STRIATION (the single most important instruction): a soft translucent cutaway running along part of the fibre reveals narrow bands that wrap around the tube and cross its ENTIRE width at every point along its length — like the printed rings on a drinking straw, the rungs of a ladder laid along the tube, or the fret markings on a drum. Each band is SHORT (spanning only the tube's width) and repeats close together along the whole visible length: a slightly deeper dusty-rose A band alternating with a paler pink I band, with a fine slate-blue Z-line at the centre of each I band, dozens of them in register, covering the whole visible length with no smooth gaps. Faint lengthwise guide-lines (the myofibrils) may show beneath the banding, but the banding itself must read as cross-bands, not as a handful of stripes running along the tube's own direction. This cross-banding covers the ENTIRE outer surface of the tube along its whole visible length, not only the cutaway strip.

Interior detail:
- along the fibre's outer rim, spaced out one after another, AT LEAST SEVEN separate oval, flattened, softly glowing blue-violet NUCLEI, every one modelled immediately under the translucent sarcolemma at the periphery — never a single nucleus in the middle;
- amber-ochre MITOCHONDRIA modelled in loose, MODERATE clusters between the myofibrils — visibly more numerous than a few scattered singles, but never a dense packed carpet — a clear middle ground;
- a modest scatter of small golden-tan GLYCOGEN GRANULES between the myofibrils, present but not dominant;
- alongside the fibre, outside the sarcolemma, one CAPILLARY with a fine crimson lumen tracking part of the fibre's length, of moderate thickness — a touch more substantial than a bare thread, but still a single slender vessel.

DO NOT model: a rounded cap or closed disc at either end; any branching, forking or Y-shape; intercalated discs; a single central nucleus or any nucleus not touching the rim; deep red or pale cream/near-white tones; a dense packed carpet of mitochondria or only one or two isolated ones; a smooth unstriated patch anywhere along the fibre; a cell wall, nucleoid, plasmids, flagella or cilia. Absolutely NO text, letters, numbers, labels, arrows, scale bars or watermarks anywhere in the image. The CAPILLARY must be a smooth, continuously curving vessel of a single uniform crimson tone throughout its length — never a candy-cane, barber-pole, segmented rod, swab stick, thermometer, or anything with alternating stripes or beads.
